\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\paragraph{The measured configuration space.}
The pool is $N=1500$ MMLU-Pro test items drawn uniformly at random, scored by Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct \citep{qwen2024} under $K=128$ configurations: four demonstration sets (none, or one of three sets of three fixed demonstrations) $\times$ two option labellings $\times$ eight instruction wordings $\times$ two answer cues.
A configuration is scored by one forward pass, the answer being the option letter with the highest log-probability after the cue; there is no sampling and no generated reasoning.
The $1500\times128$ correctness matrix is computed once, and every search, report and verification below is a replay on it.
One live check runs the \texttt{dspy} 3.0.4 optimiser \texttt{BootstrapFewShotWithRandomSearch} (Section~\ref{sec:tool}) with the same scorer as its language model; its verdict rule, that the tool's mean gap lies inside the replay's pool-bootstrap interval, was fixed before it ran.
The default (untuned) configuration is no demonstrations, ``dot'' labelling, the first wording and the first cue; it was fixed before the matrix was analysed.

\paragraph{Truth and uncertainty.}
The truth of the configuration selected on $S$ is its accuracy on the pool items outside $S$ (at least $1100$ items for $n\le400$ and $1260$ in the protocol experiments, standard error $\le0.015$); every gap is shown beside this floor.
At $n=400$ the mean gap against the items outside $S$ is $N/(N-n)=1.36$ times the gap against the whole pool, and we say which reading a point is.
The spread of the gap over draws of $S$ is reported as such; because draws from one pool overlap, every statement about behaviour on the benchmark uses a between-pool interval, from bootstrap resamples of the pool items, with the variance due to the finite number of draws of $S$ taken out of the resample spread (allowing two standard errors for its estimate) and the Monte-Carlo error of the point added, the interval placed on the point estimate (the number of resamples and draws is given in the source file of each result).
On the synthetic arm the truth is known exactly.

\paragraph{Searchers.}
\emph{Random search} evaluates $B$ configurations drawn without replacement on all of $S$; $B=K$ is the exhaustive grid.
\emph{Hill climbing} is coordinate ascent over the four factors from a random start.
\emph{Successive halving} scores all candidates on a small subset of $S$, keeps the best half and doubles the subset each round; its floor on the subset size matches its evaluation count (configuration--item cells) to that of random search at a given $B$.
Cost is counted in cells, so comparisons are at equal evaluation budget.

\paragraph{Predicting the gap from the search log.}
Let $Y_S\in\{0,1\}^{n\times B}$ be the logged item-level scores and $Z^*\sim\mathcal N(0,\hat\Sigma/n)$ a Gaussian multiplier bootstrap of their column means \citep{chernozhukov2013}.
If all configurations were equally accurate, the expected optimism of the best is $\EE\max_k Z^*_k=\kappa\,\bar\sigma/\sqrt n$, with $\bar\sigma/\sqrt n$ the mean standard error of a column; this defines $\kappa$ and the first predictor.
$\Keff$ is the number of independent standard-normal competitors whose expected maximum equals $\kappa$.
A refined predictor shrinks the scores toward their mean by an empirical-Bayes estimate of $\sigmatheta$ (Appendix~\ref{app:definitions}).

\paragraph{Reporting protocols.}
All protocols receive the same $N_{\mathrm{tot}}$ items and searcher and are scored on the fresh-item accuracy of the configuration they deploy.
\emph{Naive} reports the score on the search items.
\emph{Split} searches on a fraction $1-f$, $f\in\{0.2,0.5\}$, and reports the held-out accuracy with an exact binomial lower bound.
\emph{Nested cross-fit} (five folds) and the \emph{optimism bootstrap} (100 resamples) with its .632 variant \citep{efron1997} estimate the expected accuracy of the procedure.
\emph{Bonferroni}, \emph{\v{S}id\'ak} and \emph{max-t} give one-sided lower bounds simultaneous over a candidate set of size $m$.
Max-t, our candidate, is $\hat A_U(\chat)-q_{1-\alpha}\,\widehat{\mathrm{se}}(\chat)$, with $q_{1-\alpha}$ the $(1-\alpha)$ quantile of $\max_k Z^*_k/\widehat{\mathrm{sd}}_k$ from the logged item-level matrix, in the manner of \citet{rink2025}; it needs no held-out items and no re-search.
Bounds are formed over the configurations the search \emph{evaluated} and over the whole \emph{declared} space; for successive halving the evaluated set is the two configurations that reach the last round, a deliberately narrow set.
The level is $\alpha=0.05$.
Protocols are scored by the probability that a point report exceeds the fresh accuracy, the coverage and width of the bound, and the accuracy lost by spending items on reporting.
On the real matrix the fresh accuracy is a noisy proportion, so coverage and overstatement are scored against it and against it plus $1.645$ standard errors, and a statement is called valid or invalid only when the two scorings agree.

\paragraph{Decisions, and the matrix beyond its support.}
A claim that tuning helped is declared when the paired $z$ statistic of the tuned score against the default on the same items exceeds $1.645$ (naive), when the max-t band excludes zero (simultaneous), or on a held-out half of $S$ (split); a claim that tuned system $A$ beats tuned system $B$ compares their reported scores at equal and at four-to-one budgets.
The nulls are synthetic worlds with equal accuracies fitted to the item-level structure of the matrix, and the densest window of the real configurations in pool accuracy, a near-null.
To reach sizes and numbers of configurations the matrix cannot, a low-rank logistic model fitted to the matrix generates further worlds; its validation and its leakage probe are in Appendix~\ref{app:definitions}.
